        "hidden": [
          "wrapping",
          "size",
          "container-type",
          "container-dimensions",
          "begin-flow"
        ],
        "name": "Span - 180"
      },
      "177": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "Source\n\nWe generate a large collection of 640 random values. They are all triggered by the Metronome node at the same time. We use a Multiply node to increase the frequency of the Metronome from 0-1 to 0-16.\n\nWe read different parts of that collection by using Span nodes of varying size and convert those collections into texture. So what actually happens is that a value will range from 0 to 1. 0 is being converted to a black pixel, 1 to a white pixel and the values in between generate grayscale values. A collection of 16 values will be converted to a texture of 1x16 pixels.\nThis is miniscule, so we use the resize node to scale it up to patch size.\n\nWe use different sizes for the Span nodes to create different effects. As you can hopefully see now a texture of 1x640 pixels will generate a smoother distortion then 1x16. A Choice In node is used to switch between the different textures.\n\nThe radius slider gets mapped by the map node. The values aren’t changed, but notice the skew to the left side. This is because the bottom range of values are more sensitive for the visual result. After the Map node the signal is sent to the Max parameter on the Random node. This way we can control how light the pixels can be at maximum brightness(1 = white).\nThis is important for the next step: colorization. \n"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 388.64398193359375,
          "width": 866.0408325195312,
          "x": -2360.417724609375,
          "y": -664.6818237304688
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffff6a00",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment"
      },
      "178": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "Colorization\n\nWe take the source texture that we’ve generated and colorize it either green, red or not.\nThis is because the UV-offset node (that we’ll get to in a sec) reads red values as movement over the X-axis and green values as movement over the Y-axis.\n\nThis is why the Choice In node has the “horizontal”, “vertical” and “both” options\n"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 181.8165283203125,
          "width": 741.117431640625,
          "x": -717.327392578125,
          "y": -429.9920043945313
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffd0c117",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment"
      },
      "179": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "Rotation\n\nThis is a bit of complicated math that we’re not going to dive into, but you can just copy and paste it into any of your own patches. What this does is rather than just rotating, it scales the texture proportionally to the rotation so that the edges of the texture never show. This way you always have a full screen."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 140.51708984375,
          "width": 892.34130859375,
          "x": 672.27490234375,
          "y": -374.29888916015625
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "fff26eb5",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment"
      },
      "180": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "Blur Source\n\nA simple blur is applied to the source texture to create some more flexibility for the user, allowing for more organic distortion over a blocky one. We use Blur instead of Pixel Blur to save on GPU power as we don’t really need the detail that the Pixel Blur gives (it’s a distortion after all!)\n"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 174.7856903076172,
          "width": 480.2019348144531,
          "x": 2221.274658203125,
          "y": -408.5822448730469
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffe53357",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Comment"
      },
      "181": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": true
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "UV Distortion\n\nHere we finally bring in the input texture and distort it using the UV-Offset node.\nThe UV-offset nodes read the offset texture and offsets each pixel based on its red and green values. We give the user control over the gain to be able to distort the image beyond what the radius parameter can do.\n"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 149.13023376464844,
          "width": 703.1968994140625,
          "x": 2777.223876953125,
          "y": -396.0089111328125
        },
